{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Root cause CONFIRMED at runtime (rebar-debug Phase 1), then fixed.\n\nDiscriminating experiment on a real store (epic + 3 children):\n- baseline, all live:      signer == gate  (e3ff6fc064b016ea)\n- CONTROL, one child DELETED: signer == gate (0fb0121a21cf9e9c) - both sides already filter 'deleted', which rules out 'the two enumerations diverge generally'\n- TREATMENT, one child ARCHIVED: signer = 0fb0121a21cf9e9c (UNCHANGED - still counts it), gate = 1ffdcb3d79bd745a (dropped it) -> NOT EQUAL\n\nsnapshot.child_ids still contained the archived child. Exactly the predicted-if-true outcome.\n\nFIX: a single shared predicate relation_snapshot.counts_as_plan_material_child (live only: excludes archived + deleted). The gate now enumerates WIDE (include_archived=True) and narrows through that one helper, so the two sides cannot diverge by construction. AC1 satisfied structurally.\n\nIMPORTANT - AC1 and AC3 as written are MUTUALLY UNSATISFIABLE. AC3 asks that archiving a child leave the container's material fingerprint unchanged. But once archived children are excluded from the basis (AC1's 'live only'), archiving one necessarily removes it from the basis and so necessarily MOVES the fingerprint. The ticket's rationale had this inverted: it is the *include-archived* option that would leave the fingerprint untouched, not the exclude option that was chosen.\n\nResolved in favour of AC1 (the structural, primary criterion). The regression test now pins the invariant that actually cures the bug - signer and gate agree before AND after the archive - and explicitly records the accepted residual cost: archiving a child invalidates the container's attestation and requires a fresh review. That is recoverable, unlike the permanent unclaimable loop this bug caused. AC3 should be amended.\n\nVerification: RED->GREEN with the test held out from the implementing agent; defect-seeded mutation (helper reverted to status != 'deleted') returns both tests to RED. The reporter's exact loop (epic + archived child -> review-plan -> claim) is now a permanent regression test in tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_plan_review_gate.py and reproduces the original failure when the fix is reverted."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785345074065890001,"uuid":"721f8a8c-e768-4b6f-ba2a-86ecf9a2fbc4"}